    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWe can use place value strategies to divide some large numbers quickly and efficiently.\n\nContent:\nTo demonstrate, let's discuss how to use place value strategies to solve the following division problem:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: {\\color{red}{20}} \\div 2]\n\nContent:\nFirst, we write the number we're dividing [MATH: ({\\color{red}{20}})] in expanded form:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2 \u00d7 10 \u00f7 2]\n\nContent:\nThen, we swap the order of the multiplication and division and evaluate the expression:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2 \u00d7 10 \u00f7 2|=; 2 \u00f7 2 \u00d7 10|=; (2 \u00f7 2) \u00d7 10|=; 1 \u00d7 10|=; 10]\n\nContent:\nTherefore,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 20 \\div 2 = 10.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Dividing Two-Digit Numbers by One-Digit Numbers\n\nExample Prompt:\nSarah will use [MATH: 90] beads to make [MATH: 3] identical necklaces. How many beads will each necklace have?\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo determine the number of beads each necklace will have, we need to calculate [MATH: 90\\div 3.]\n\nRemember that [MATH: 90] in expanded form is\n\n[MATH: 9 \\times 10.]\n\nTherefore,\n\n[MATH: 90 \u00f7 3|=; 9 \u00d7 10 \u00f7 3|.]\n\nThen, we swap the order of the multiplication and division:\n\n[MATH: 9 \u00d7 10 \u00f7 3|=; 9 \u00f7 3 \u00d7 10|=; (9 \u00f7 3) \u00d7 10|=; 3 \u00d7 10|=; 30]\n\nTherefore, each necklace will have [MATH: 30] beads.\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 80 \u00f7 8 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 40]\nb. [MATH: 4]\nc. [MATH: 10]\nd. [MATH: 8]\ne. [MATH: 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 80 \u00f7 2 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 40]\nb. [MATH: 4]\nc. [MATH: 10]\nd. [MATH: 8]\ne. [MATH: 20]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Dividing Three-Digit Numbers by One-Digit Numbers\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is [MATH: 280 \\div 7?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nRemember that [MATH: 280] is the same as [MATH: 28] tens. So, we have\n\n[MATH: 280 = 28 \\times {10}.]\n\nTherefore,\n\n[MATH: 280 \u00f7 7|=; 28 \u00d7 10 \u00f7 7|.]\n\nThen, we swap the order of the multiplication and division:\n\n[MATH: 28 \u00d7 10 \u00f7 7|=; 28 \u00f7 7 \u00d7 10|=; (28 \u00f7 7) \u00d7 10|=; 4 \u00d7 10|=; 40]\n\nTherefore, [MATH: 280\\div 7 = 40.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 160 \u00f7 8 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 24]\nb. [MATH: 18]\nc. [MATH: 20]\nd. [MATH: 16]\ne. [MATH: 22]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 500 \u00f7 2 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 50]\nb. [MATH: 200]\nc. [MATH: 25]\nd. [MATH: 150]\ne. [MATH: 250]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Dividing Four-Digit Numbers by One-Digit Numbers\n\nExample Prompt:\nCompute the value of [MATH: 3,200 \\div 4.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nRemember that [MATH: 3,200] is the same as [MATH: 32] hundreds. So, we have\n\n[MATH: 3,200 = 32 \\times {100}.]\n\nTherefore,\n\n[MATH: 3, 200 \u00f7 4|=; 32 \u00d7 100 \u00f7 4|.]\n\nThen, we swap the order of the multiplication and division:\n\n[MATH: 32 \u00d7 100 \u00f7 4|=; 32 \u00f7 4 \u00d7 100|=; (32 \u00f7 4) \u00d7 100|=; 8 \u00d7 100|=; 800]\n\nTherefore, [MATH: 3,200\\div 4 = 800.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 5, 500 \u00f7 5 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 550]\nb. [MATH: 100]\nc. [MATH: 110]\nd. [MATH: 1, 100]\ne. [MATH: 1, 000]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nMaria plans to give her [MATH: 6] grandchildren an equal share of [MATH: $4, 200] as a gift when she retires. How much money will each grandchild receive?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: $800]\nb. [MATH: $700]\nc. [MATH: $900]\nd. [MATH: $740]\ne. [MATH: $850]"
